Defer in a not-taken branch does not fire
=========================================

`defer` semantics are **dynamic**, not lexical: a `defer stmt`
queues a cleanup only if control actually reaches the
statement. If the branch containing the `defer` is never
entered at runtime, the deferred body must NOT run at function
exit.

For a long time every codegen backend (llvm-host, svm-host,
trisc, riscv64, riscv32, wasm32) treated `defer` lexically —
one cleanup site per textual `defer` regardless of dynamic
reachability — so the deferred body fired even in skipped
branches. Only the interpreter honored dynamic queuing. The
fix is the per-defer-site counter scheme: at runtime each
defer-site has an i64 counter, the defer-statement codegen
emits a `counter++`, and the fn-exit codegen replays the body
`counter` times inside a `while counter > 0` loop. A skipped
branch leaves the counter at 0; the loop runs 0 iterations;
the body never fires.

See `feedback_sysl_codegen_defer_in_skipped_branch.md`.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct DsbTrace
        s: string

Branch-conditional defer with both paths
----------------------------------------

A single helper takes a `bool` switch. When the switch is
true the defer queues and fires at fn exit; when false the
defer is never queued and must not fire.

    dsb_branch(t: *DsbTrace, take: bool) -> unit
        if take
            defer t.s = t.s + "B"
            t.s = t.s + "x"
        t.s = t.s + "y"

    #test
    test_taken_branch_fires_defer() -> unit
        var t = DsbTrace("")
        dsb_branch(&t, true)
        assert(t.s == "xyB", s"expected 'xyB' on taken branch, got '${t.s}'")

    #test
    test_skipped_branch_does_not_fire_defer() -> unit
        var t = DsbTrace("")
        dsb_branch(&t, false)
        assert(t.s == "y", s"expected 'y' on skipped branch (no defer), got '${t.s}'")

Two branches, only one's defer fires
------------------------------------

If/else where each arm has its own `defer`. Whichever arm
runs queues its defer; the other's is never queued.

    dsb_two_branches(t: *DsbTrace, pick: int) -> unit
        if pick == 1
            defer t.s = t.s + "1"
            t.s = t.s + "a"
        else
            defer t.s = t.s + "2"
            t.s = t.s + "b"

    #test
    test_two_branches_pick_first() -> unit
        var t = DsbTrace("")
        dsb_two_branches(&t, 1)
        assert(t.s == "a1", s"expected 'a1' (then-branch only), got '${t.s}'")

    #test
    test_two_branches_pick_second() -> unit
        var t = DsbTrace("")
        dsb_two_branches(&t, 7)
        assert(t.s == "b2", s"expected 'b2' (else-branch only), got '${t.s}'")

Early-return path skips post-defer
----------------------------------

A defer queued before an early return must still fire on the
early-return path; a defer queued *after* the early return
must not fire on that path. This pins that counters increment
strictly at the defer-statement source location, not at the
top of the function.

    dsb_early_return(t: *DsbTrace, x: int) -> int
        defer t.s = t.s + "A"
        if x == 0
            return -1
        defer t.s = t.s + "B"
        return x

    #test
    test_early_return_only_first_defer() -> unit
        var t = DsbTrace("")
        val r = dsb_early_return(&t, 0)
        assert(r == -1, s"expected -1, got $r")
        assert(t.s == "A", s"expected 'A' only (B was after early return), got '${t.s}'")

    #test
    test_fall_through_both_defers() -> unit
        var t = DsbTrace("")
        val r = dsb_early_return(&t, 7)
        assert(r == 7, s"expected 7, got $r")
        // LIFO: A queued first, B queued second; exit fires B then A.
        assert(t.s == "BA", s"expected 'BA' (LIFO), got '${t.s}'")
